186435019 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435019 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "73c99f01-9858-5d97-8611-2fe539762d19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99037" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435022 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=25-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24aab5f2-d53f-5124-b5bc-fe883979c5fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435022 #edb/ref 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=\\lambda (2y-4)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1+\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(1+\\lambda ) &=2\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0,$ the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,4).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1,$ from the second equation of the system we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1-1)=2(-1)\\,⇒\\,0=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $25$ | $9$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1045 "Lagrange Multipliers With One Constraint" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186483465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482810 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "da0cbfc1-126a-53bb-9e82-05a1649265b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482822 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482822 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482822 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "077e60b6-8dfd-5425-af73-cfd611ef7918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482839 #edb/ref 1080 "-9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482839 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "112e5b58-a689-5138-b33f-e47c56e7167c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482914 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482914 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482914 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3c313004-620