a ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,1),\\,(2,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(2,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-9$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483437 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f702bf3-bb64-5422-aea8-b0bbbff9042c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483437 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483444 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483444 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f621894-6c34-5b5d-bbbc-0b80620d0a44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483444 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186372202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483465 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483465 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61c5b431